{
  "dom_id": "step-q318223",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/551c38c3bedd56d8fc1c4fef9f1fcc15b9fbf6fa83af81253b7a4d2296aaeb38.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-1]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1]$, the three line segments for $x\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6+3+7 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
